style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:2.5cm" alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img data-src="../../images/attack2.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:2.5cm" alt="image" /></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img data-src="../../images/attack3.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:2.5cm" alt="image" /></td>
<td style="text-align: center;"><img data-src="../../images/attack4.png"
style="max-width: none; width: auto; height:2.5cm" alt="image" /></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
A reminder of the types of attacks
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>The diagrams above, of Interception, Modification, Interruption, and
Fabrication, remind us of the routes taken.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
<section>
<section id="fighting-back" class="title-slide slide level1">
<h1>Fighting back</h1>

</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Its not just the Internet</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Othernets</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">The radio spectrum</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Fighting
back</span></span>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-bold">Awareness, evaluation,
consideration</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Padding oracle attack for Lab2</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Padding, release of partial
information</span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Evaluation<span class="hfill-right"> <span>[</span><a
href="https://hugh.myddns.me/media/pdfs/Maroochy-Water-Services-Case-Study_report.pdf">ctrl-i3</a>,<a
href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-53r5.pdf">X</a><span>]</span></span></h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
It is not magic:
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>We should evaluate and manage this level of our systems in the same
way as we assess and control our computer systems.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Assuming we had a system that made use of communication
infrastructure, we should <span style="color: blue">evaluate</span> it’s
security, do a <span style="color: blue">risk assessment</span>, and
ensure we have appropriate <span
style="color: blue">controls</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
NIST Special Publication 800-53
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>For security controls for all U.S. federal information systems. It’s
approach seems relevant to assessing any security issue, including ones
like this. The framework includes:</p>
</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Technical issues </span> include access control,
audit and accountability, identification and authentication, and system
and communication protection.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Operational issues </span> include awareness and
training, contingency planning, incident response, maintenance and
physical protection, and even personnel.</span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: blue">Management issues</span> include certification, risk
assessment and so on.</span></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Summary: Other networks</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Today, we have seen...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">A range</span> - We were reminded that the
“Internet” and “IP protocols” are not the only communication mechanisms
that we, and our computers, rely on. Many of our essential services
(electricity, water, sewage) are still using ancient protocols and
techniques. In addition, our factories, the GPS system, our phones,
dongles to drive cars and so on, are all using communication methods and
hardware designed in many cases 50 years or so ago.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Particular Nets</span> - Specifically, we
looked at ADS-B - the flight location broadcast system, GSM and LTE MiM
attacks, messing about with NFC, and GPS - the global positioning
system.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: blue">Fightback</span> - The view here is that
none of these systems require us to invent new ways of dealing with
them. We can just start with an awareness that they exist, and then use
standard evaluation and management tools. We saw the use of NIST 800-53
as a framework for analysis.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
<section>
<section id="padding-oracle-attack-for-lab2"
class="title-slide slide level1">
<h1>Padding oracle attack for Lab2</h1>

</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Outline</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="dada-toc">
<ol>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Its not just the Internet</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Othernets</span></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">The radio spectrum</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Fighting back</span>
<ul>
<li><span style="color: lightgray">Awareness, evaluation,
consideration</span></li>
</ul></li>
<li><span style="color: blue"><span class="dada-bold">Padding oracle
attack for Lab2</span></span>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-bold">Padding, release of partial
information</span></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Bit and byte padding for block ciphers...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-1.png" style="height: auto; width:60.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="exampleblock">
<div class="block-title">
PKCS #7 - a byte-wise padding technique...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>One technique is to add a single 1 bit, followed by enough 0 bits to
fill out the block.</p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p>Another is the PKCS #7 paddings for the final block. Each byte is
given as 2 hex digits.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>CBC: encoding and decoding...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/cbc2.png" style="height: auto; width:80.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="center">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">(...decode is </span><span
style="color: blue">decrypt, then XOR</span><span
style="color: black">).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
... and encode is XOR, then encrypt...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Note that when you are decoding, the final
plaintext</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">p_{2}</span></span><span style="color: black"> is
the decrypted ciphertext exclusive-ored with</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">If there was only one block, the final
plaintext</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">p_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> is
the decrypted ciphertext exclusive-ored with</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">IV</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Padding oracle...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7Attack.png" style="height: auto; width:40.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
What is a padding oracle?
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Imagine that you have a bit of software,
that, given</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black">
and</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{2}</span></span><span style="color: black">, will
tell you if the padding is correct in</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">p_{2}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. If the message was only one block long,
given</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">IV</span></span><span style="color: black">
and</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> (as
in the lab), it will tell you if the padding is correct in</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">p_{1}</span></span><span
style="color: black">. In general, </span><span
style="color: blue">given <span class="math inline">c_{n-1}</span> and
<span class="math inline">c_{n}</span>, it will tell you if the padding
is correct. </span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Is this </span><span
style="color: blue">realistic</span><span style="color: black">? Well,
</span><span style="color: blue">yes</span><span style="color: black">
it is. When you send a padded, encrypted message to another system,
after decrypting, the other system will check the padding, and if it is
not valid, will fail immediately. Otherwise it will fail (or succeed)
later.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Normal receiving software may leak the
correctness of the padding. </span><span style="color: blue">In the lab,
we will give you a program that is a padding oracle.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Note also that an </span><span
style="color: blue">you can directly change each bit/byte of the final
plaintext for <span class="math inline">p_{n}</span>,</span><span
style="color: black"> because the attacker has control over the
input</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{n-1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> (or
IV as in the lab).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-2.png" style="height: auto; width:75.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
The attacker does not get to see the decrypted data...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">The oracle only learns if the last bytes
are correct - they could be </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>01</code></span></span><span
style="color: blue">, </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>0202</code></span></span><span
style="color: blue">, </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>030303</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">, and so on. </span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">The attacker starts by trying a series of
values for the last byte of</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span
style="color: black">:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-3.png" style="height: auto; width:75.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Attacker discovers last byte...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">The attacker is trying values in the last
byte of </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> until
</span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>01</code></span></span><span
style="color: black"> is the last byte. Oracle returns OK.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Attacker now sets the last byte
to</span><span style="color: blue"> </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>02</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">, and tries for the second-last byte of</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span
style="color: black">:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-4.png" style="height: auto; width:75.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Attacker discovers second-to-last byte...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Since we know (now) the last byte
(</span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>02</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">), and the original last byte of</span><span
style="color: blue"> <span class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span
style="color: black">, we can set the last byte.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">We try all the second-to-last values
in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black">, and
uncover a second byte.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-5.png" style="height: auto; width:75.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Attacker discovers third-to-last byte...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Since we know (now) the last two bytes
(</span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>02 02</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">), we can set the last two bytes to</span><span
style="color: blue"> </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>03 03</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Now we try all the third-to-last values
in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> and
uncover</span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: black"><code> </code></span><span
style="color: blue"><code>20</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="center">
<p><img data-src="../../images/pkcs7-6.png" style="height: auto; width:75.0%"
alt="image" /></p>
</div>
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Attacker discovers fourth-to-last byte...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Since we know (now) the last three bytes
(</span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>20 02 02</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">), we can set the last three bytes to</span><span
style="color: blue"> </span><span class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>04 04 04</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">Now we try all the fourth-to-last values
in</span><span style="color: blue"> <span
class="math inline">c_{1}</span></span><span style="color: black"> and
uncover</span><span style="color: blue"> </span><span
class="dada-bold"><span
style="color: blue"><code>6f</code></span></span><span
style="color: black">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="slide level2">
<h2>Using the oracle in an attack...</h2>
<div class="dada-frame-body">
<div class="block">
<div class="block-title">
Attacker discovers all bytes...
</div>
<div class="block-content">
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">The attacker keeps moving through the
block, and has to try a maximum of </span><span
style="color: blue">255</span><span style="color: black"> trials for
each byte recovered, so this is an</span><span style="color: blue">
<span class="math inline">{\cal O}(n)</span> attack</span><span
style="color: black">, where </span><span style="color: blue"><span
class="math inline">n</span></span><span style="color: black"> is the
number of bytes in the block.</span></p>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<div style="color: black">
<hr style="border: none; border-top: 3px solid black; width: 100%; margin: 0.4em 0;">
</div>
</div>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">This attack </span><span
style="color: blue">can be avoided</span><span style="color: black">
if...</span></p>
</div>
<ul>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span
style="color: black">There is no oracle (i.e. the receiving code does
not give any indication if the padding is correct or
not)</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">CFB
is used instead.</span></span></li>
<li><span class="dada-font-footnotesize"><span style="color: black">A
different padding scheme is used...</span></span></li>
</ul>
<div class="dada-font-footnotesize">
<p><span style="color: black">That is it - a little aside into cracking
that makes use of much of what we have learned.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section></section>
    </div>
  </div>

